I've implemented the node within its declared grant (`http-client` only) and left out the `state-rw` import and the `last-input` write. The graph's `with` clause is the component's entire authority. A component that imports an interface the graph doesn't grant would either fail to link against the host or put the binary out of step with the definition the validator checks. That would be true even for a short time. Once the next release adds `StateHandle<…, read-write>` to `FetchFeeds`, regenerate the node and I'll add the `last-input` write then. The node's output is unchanged either way.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{subscription-list, feed-batch};

    import aap:caps/http-client@0.1.0;

    export run: func(subscriptions: subscription-list) -> feed-batch;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::http_client;
use crate::aap::caps::types::{FeedDocument, FetchFailure};

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(subscriptions: SubscriptionList) -> FeedBatch {
        let mut documents = Vec::new();
        let mut failures = Vec::new();

        for url in subscriptions.urls {
            match http_client::fetch(&url) {
                Ok(body) => documents.push(FeedDocument { url, body }),
                Err(error) => failures.push(FetchFailure { url, error }),
            }
        }

        FeedBatch {
            documents,
            failures,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

